**Definition 13.2 (native ingress and import).** Port 38 has an F sentinel.
Every subsequent ingress frame is one immutable binary block followed by
F. From installed Ready execute the client-to-handler service-token TOK,
then the reserve CTL, for source and service tags alike. Busy or missing-token
attempts have no executed successor. Before its first IN, Base(38) records
the actual next base. Each of three tag bits executes IN(latch 6); EX(38);
WR(38,latch 6); MR(38). Length reception executes IN(6); EX(38);
WR(38,6); RD(38,5); MR(38), repeating on 1 and ending on 0.
Thus latch 6 survives its matching write, and RD's test latch is distinct.

Use the recorded Base offset with the full Seek to the new frame, skip
its three header cells, and copy the real $U(d)$ to length scratch. Seal
that scratch word, position it by LastScratch, and Tail(38). For each
leading 1 use RD(length,5); IN(6); EX(38); WR(38,6); MR(38);
MR(length). The terminal RD(0) exits. This payload body has $6d+1$
occurrences. It uses append initialization, rather than a preallocated
AIF payload; hence $5d+1$ is not its price. The following F append and
frame seal are four more occurrences.

Construct $U(2d+4)$ on Base scratch 34, distinct from its source length
word on 35. Tail(34); append four 1s; LastScratch(35,0); for each leading
1 execute RD(35); Append(34,1); Append(34,1); MR(35), with the terminal
RD(0) exiting; append 0, sep and seal on 34. Its body after the two
positionings costs $8d+20$. Descriptor creation then counts port 36's
preceding descriptor Fs, appending $U(c)$ on 34. No other Base-scratch
word is appended in between. Its last three words are therefore base,
measured frame length and creation ordinal: LastScratch offsets 2, 1 and
0. Copy these to the descriptor with literal owner/port and flags 10;
append F and seal. Its actual payload length is $2d+4$. In particular
this length construction never tries to traverse and append on the same
ordinary head. All source/length/scratch positions use §11's scans;
no empty-port price is used.

Import of this sealed frame uses LastBank(38), now scanning its binary
payload between Fs. Clear a word register, RD at each payload cell; on
0 or 1 choose the corresponding fixed cons assignment, then MR(38).
At F stop and Reverse that register. Word assignments never touch port
38, so their scans cannot lose this actual source position. The RD,
finite bit test, complete bank assignment and MR are all paid. This
specifies the import's expansion, rather than an instruction that reads
a finite string in one step. Installation-manifest import uses the same
routine on its actual sealed field blocks on port 37.

At handler entry completed-frames still denotes exactly the completed
predecessor frames. The current frame has already physically sealed on
38, but is appended to that word only on common frame return. Thus Cover
freezes the preceding Ready, before this frame's admission work, rather
than a point selected later in its own computation. The entrance is the last post-acknowledgement Ready before its
reserve/admission CTL, as specified in Definition 7.1.
The distinction pays for admission and fixes its actual target boundary.

A metadata word on scratch 34 or 35 is not imported while the bank
compiler is changing that same scratch. Use the following native staging
bridge whenever a physical descriptor/length must enter a word register.
Port 37 is an append stream of immutable fields; its installation manifest
is a frozen prefix, not a variable meaning its latest block.

For `Stage34(e)`, Tail(34), Tail(37) and Base(37) append its new base to 34, thereby
increasing the saved source word's backwards offset from $e$ to $e+1$.
Tail(35); construct its actual bit length there as $1U(n)$, using one literal 1
and a unary copy from that saved $U(n)$ on 34, then sep and seal.
Reposition that saved word and copy it bitwise to 37; append F and seal.
Count descriptors, then write this staged block's full descriptor with
its new base at offset 1 on 34, length on 35 and ordinal at offset 0.
For `Stage35(e)`, Tail(34); Base(37) again writes its base on 34, while the source
word on 35 does not move to a different record. Construct $1U(n)$ on 34
from that source by literal 1 plus unary copy; copy the original source
to 37 and seal; append the creation ordinal on 34. Its base, length and
ordinal now have offsets 2, 1 and 0, all on 34. Copy them to its descriptor.
All source, count and destination ports in these recipes are distinct.

Import the resulting latest sealed binary block from 37 with the same
RD/cons/MR/Reverse loop used on 38. Assignment helpers never touch 37.
At initialization use the following full-prefix graph `ManifestPrefix(37)`
instead. Its input is the complete sealed installation prefix, before any
staged fields have been appended. Its first ordinary cell is the actual F
sentinel created by $L$, distinct from every field's following F. Initialize
the output-list register by $f:=\epsilon;\ f:=0f$, the encoded empty list.
Then execute **Home(37); MR(37); MR(37)**. Home ends at L; the first move
reaches the sentinel and the second reaches the first field, its delimiter
if empty, or R if there are no fields. Neither move performs a symbol test
or a word assignment. The sentinel is never passed to the field loop.

| Native label | Actual row/word expansion and successor |
| --- | --- |
| BeginField | RD(37,4), testing R. On R go to Done. Otherwise execute the compiled assignment $t:=\epsilon$, then go to FieldRead. |
| FieldRead | RD(37,4), testing F. On F go to FinishField. Otherwise execute CTL testing latch 4 for 0; on 0 go to Bit0. Otherwise execute CTL testing that same latch for 1; on 1 go to Bit1, otherwise take internal-description failure. |
| Bit0 / Bit1 | Execute the compiled literal assignment $t:=0t$ / $t:=1t$, then one MR(37) and return to FieldRead. |
| FinishField | Reverse($t$) into a fresh word temporary; AppendItem($f$,that word) into $f$; execute one MR(37), then go to BeginField. |
| Done | Execute the compiled copy $\mathrm{manifest}:=f$ and continue Initialize. |

The CTLs in FieldRead have no ordinary-cell effect. Their successful bit
branch selects a literal cons before any bank helper can overwrite latch 4.
BeginField's latch is not reused after its clearing assignment: FieldRead
performs its own new RD. Reverse and AppendItem have Definition 12.1's
acyclic expansions, not native string instructions. Their finite exits,
like the bit assignments' exits, carry the resulting actual bank parity.
The compiler uses the finite product with that parity, not a return stack.

Port 37 is not extended during this scan. There is no variable ordinal
lookup before its record has been imported. The native fields have
Definition 14.2's fixed order; lists within a field use their explicit
wire grammar. The final saved-head-table field has its own F. A field's
F always emits one word, including an empty word; only R at BeginField
ends the list. The old-frame list itself uses its explicit terminal 0.
Only after full import may channel staging append further blocks to 37.
Manifest lookup thereafter is in the immutable bank word, not latest-37.
The physical descriptor words have thereby entered actual word-register
cells, with their provenance. No word assignment assumes a mathematical
length or reads scratch through a head that its own bank rebuild moves.

For channel positioning, save the sender, mailbox and receiver descriptor
words as three native staged records on 37 immediately upon their creation,
before the next Base or bank operation. A fixed finite backwards-F scan
selects the third, second or last of those blocks. Within one descriptor,
skip the fixed number of unary fields by RD; MR through its 0, and copy
the desired base/length field to distinct scratch by the complete unary
copy. Seek uses that actually copied base; a copy loop uses that actually
copied length. These three records remain the latest three throughout the
native setup/copy sequence; no bank assignment or other staging occurs
inside it. Before mailbox allocation, read the sender staged descriptor's actual
length field into 35; before receiver allocation, read that same field
from what is then the second-last staged block. Thus staging's own measured
wire length is never reused as the payload length. A staged descriptor
itself is copied from the latest complete
port-36 record, using the analogous backward-F scan, so measuring its
new stage Base does not destroy its source fields. Staging has its own
Base, measured payload length, full descriptor, F and seal. The last
three rule specifies records in order sender, mailbox, receiver, not
unacquired native addresses. After that native sequence, banks are
unchanged and the word programme resumes at its preserved finite parity.
The cost grammar of §11 applies to every one of these scans and appends.

The native descriptor staging just used has a concrete two-pass body.
Tail(34); Base(37) first records its destination base. Locate the latest
port-36 descriptor by Tail(36); ML; ML and backward RD/ML until the
preceding F or L; MR gives its first bit. Tail(35); scan its bits through
but excluding its terminal F, appending one 1 on 35 per bit; append 0,
sep and seal on 35. Reposition that same port-36 descriptor, Tail(37),
and copy each bit by RD(36,4); Append(37,latch 4); MR(36); exit on F.
Append F and seal on 37. Only now count the old descriptor Fs and append
this staging block's full descriptor. Thus its descriptor source is
unchanged throughout both passes; measuring the stage never moves its
source through an overwritten scratch head. Staging a descriptor does
not recursively stage its newly created staging descriptor.

When allocating mailbox or receiver, flags 11 describe the initialized,
still writable interval during copying. Those setup descriptors and their
staged copies are historical placement records, not claims that a sealed
receiver is currently writable. Before final availability the native
controller appends a sealed completion record: copy the setup's actual
owner/port/base/length/origin, its completed seal kind and flags 10.
It uses the same native staged-field unary copies and literal appends,
with no bank assignment. Completion records go on port 39 as tagged
binary records with Base/length/descriptor/F/seal, after their own dry
count and real copy. They are not new staging blocks on 37, so the three
placement records retain their positions throughout both copy stages.
The destination's live token/seal state has that same completed successor;
no current permission is inferred from an older flag 11. These additional
records, copies, tests and seals belong to the channel cost, beyond its
$12l+6$ two-body subtotal.

Before either payload copy, recover both endpoint bases from the three
staged placement records and Seek each actual endpoint. Then copy the
sender's payload length afresh from its staged record to 35 and position
that latest word with LastScratch(35,0). Earlier allocations and descriptor
staging changed this scratch, so its old head or latest value is never
reused as though still the payload counter. The six-row body alone then
advances its endpoints and this counter. No staging operation occurs
inside it.

